   "tutor": "Here is the rule: for something happening now, use am, is or are plus the verb with -ing.\nRight: \"At the zoo, the monkey is eating a banana.\" Wrong: \"At the zoo, the monkey eating a banana.\" The wrong one leaves out is, so the -ing verb has no helper.\nLet me think aloud with one example: \"At the zoo, a lion is sleeping.\" Step 1: who? A lion, one animal, so is. Step 2: what is it doing now? Sleep, plus -ing.\nNow your turn. Write your own, different sentences about a new place:\nImagine a park. Write two sentences about what people are doing now.",
   "raw": "[[assess verdict=NOT_EVALUATED relevance=NO_ACTIVE_ITEM next=NEW_ITEM item=w1]]\nHere is the rule: for something happening now, use am, is or are plus the verb with -ing.\nRight: \"At the zoo, the monkey is eating a banana.\" Wrong: \"At the zoo, the monkey eating a banana.\" The wrong one leaves out is, so the -ing verb has no helper.\nLet me think aloud with one example: \"At the zoo, a lion is sleeping.\" Step 1: who? A lion, one animal, so is. Step 2: what is it doing now? Sleep, plus -ing.\nNow your turn. Write your own, different sentences about a new place:\nImagine a park. Write two sentences about what people are doing now.",
